92186367921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124596 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1045 "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124734 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "203caeae-670f-5f01-89d7-10fbe0661dd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125039 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125039 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125039 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125039 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125039 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0722c4ed-9b45-5a82-9aca-63ef2538a12b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125039 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125040 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125040 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125040 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125040 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08b52ee2-9e81-5a5f-aeda-8fc812032d41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125040 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125243 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6edb4185-988d-5465-a73c-d87078eb5744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125243 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125531 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125531 #edb/ref 1061 30975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125531 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "683354ae-e60a-5bdb-b374-4caf6c7d6268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125531 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125532 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125532 #edb/ref 1061 30976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125532 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "216ca278-c544-5255-95a8-27a607e680f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125532 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1061 30977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dba6958-5536-5be1-b625-df1a632c257a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125534 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125534 #edb/ref 1061 30978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125534 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4acd445-680f-5872-a4a3-6bda4e7738eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125534 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125535 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125535 #edb/ref 1061 30979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125535 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19fc48bd-f02a-5fc4-8b9f-23e3d17412b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125535 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125562 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125562 #edb/ref 1061 33488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "443067c1-4f4c-574c-818f-cec45ee26f17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125563 #edb/ref 1061 33489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125563 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64b76ae8-f6a1-53fa-b321-5f096bee3ed4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125563 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125837 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a function $f(x,y),$ how do we calculate the partial derivatives $f_r$ and $f_\\theta,$ where $(r,\\theta)$ are plane polar coordinates?\n\nTo do this, we can use the multivariable chain rule. First, let's write down our tree diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f19c4a682b30f1eea1d9c1be7da83bd77ba137173f25eb9c7e5cf253abba015.png)\n\nFrom the tree diagram, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take the partial derivatives of $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the multivariable chain rule, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125837 #edb/ref 1065 4974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125837 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1b053bdf-59d9-5c48-bb1e-43f776bb9b90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125837 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125838 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a function of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ we can find formulas for $f_r, f_\\theta,$ and $f_z,$ where $(r,\\theta,z)$ are cylindrical polar coordinates. The results that we need are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first and second equations are identical to the equations derived for plane polar coordinates, as we would expect. \n\nThe difference here is that we now have a third equation that deals with the $z$-dependence. But since the $z$-coordinate is the same in both Cartesian and cylindrical polar coordinates, we can simply differentiate with respect to $z$ as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125838 #edb/ref 1065 4975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125838 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "733b56f3-7c9a-59a6-bc6b-ef87bf07d149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125838 #edb/ref 1067 "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125847 #edb/ref 1064 "Up to now, we've been using the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{r} & ={f}_{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {f}_{\\theta } & =-r{f}_{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can treat the above as a system of equations in the \"unknowns\" $f_x$ and $f_y.$ Solving this system gives explicit expressions for $f_x$ and $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$\n\nFor example, suppose we want an explicit formula for $f_x.$ We can eliminate $f_y$ by multiplying the first equation by $r\\cos\\theta$ and the second equation by $\\sin\\theta$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r{f}_{r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =r{f}_{x}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {f}_{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-r{f}_{x}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\nr f_r \\cos\\theta - f_\\theta \\sin\\theta = r f_x(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nf_x = f_r \\cos\\theta - \\dfrac1r f_\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can use a similar procedure to write $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$ Our results as summarized below:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\cos \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin \\theta\\[1ex]\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\sin \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\cos \\theta\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125847 #edb/ref 1065 5358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125847 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3237863-f115-5396-a03f-fd481d101761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125847 #edb/ref 1067 "Differentiating of a Function Given in Polar Coordinates With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125848 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have a function $f(x,y,z),$ and we wish to find formulas for $f_\\rho,$ $f_\\theta,$ and $f_\\phi$, where $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ are spherical polar coordinates.\n\nUsing the multivariable chain rule, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\rho } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂ϕ} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might want to try deriving these formulas for yourself!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125848 #edb/ref 1065 5359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125848 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d956ec78-e77b-5d2c-8e7a-97ff2e549005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125848 #edb/ref 1067 "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Spherical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357819 #edb/ref 1080 "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357819 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07a1ee6a-caac-58ee-afb5-38bc8bb1ed51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357907 #edb/ref 1080 "{r}^{2}(2z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12192e9d-666e-5c58-ae9b-549a795d8bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357911 #edb/ref 1080 "8z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "129511e1-8153-5e51-95ae-9c26ed91e829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358049 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358049 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358049 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2228fe89-b437-5a7a-aa61-67383a3bc1cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358054 #edb/ref 1080 "8z+{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358054 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358054 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22e04b8b-94b1-5f23-a25d-237f08e39a4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358058 #edb/ref 1080 "2{r}^{2}(z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358058 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358058 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2313e01f-d71c-5200-a515-ee826a71b2c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358077 #e