coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360565 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a5ea0e8-c2a8-56bd-bc0a-1183f811a786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360565 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360567 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360567 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360567 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09b6c584-862f-5f2c-8d59-a960b719bfdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360567 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360573 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4ca4975-1827-563d-9fb6-1ff2551b2f31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360573 #edb/ref 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }=-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360575 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360575 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360575 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360575 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $w(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360575 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0702360a-d22f-5e34-a49e-3610cfb8aefb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360575 #edb/ref 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
